    <sys:String x:Key="Str_Help">Help</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_HelpTitle">Quick Guide</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_HelpUsageHeader">Using the App</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_HelpUsageStep1">1. Create a new project or import an existing spotter pack (spmsg.txt).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_HelpUsageStep2">2. For each spotter message, enter the text and choose an audio source: select a Google TTS voice, or record/import your own audio via the Takes window.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_HelpUsageStep3">3. Optionally translate messages, adjust phrasing, and enable radio/squelch effects.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_HelpUsageStep4">4. Mark messages with a review status as you go.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_HelpUsageStep5">5. Enter a pack name and click Export to generate the final pack folder (containing the generated WAV files plus a spmsg.txt), ready to be copied into your iRacing installation.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_HelpActivateHeader">Activating a Spotter Pack in iRacing</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_HelpActivateStep1">1. Locate your iRacing installation folder (e.g. C:\iRacing) and its sound\spcc\ subfolder. This folder already exists by default.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_HelpActivateStep2">2. Copy the exported pack folder entirely into that sound\spcc\ folder of your iRacing installation.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_HelpActivateStep3">3. Make sure the folder contains the generated .wav files together with spmsg.txt.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_HelpActivateStep4">4. In iRacing, open the audio settings and select your new pack under "AI Spotter Options - Voice Pack".</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_HelpActivateStep5">5. Restart iRacing so the selected voice pack is loaded.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_HelpActivateTip">Tip: keep a backup of your project .json file so you can reopen it later in the generator to tweak messages or add new ones without starting over.</sys:String>

    <!-- Inline settings panel -->
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_GoogleApiKeyHeader">Google Cloud Text-to-Speech API Key</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_GoogleApiKeyHint">Create an API key in the Google Cloud Console (the Text-to-Speech API must be enabled). The key is stored locally in %AppData%\iRacingSpotterGenerator\settings.json (unencrypted).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_LinkCreateApiKey">Create API Key</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_LinkEnableTts">Enable Text-to-Speech API</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_LinkEnableTranslate">Enable Translation API</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_DefaultVoiceHeader">Default AI Voice (Google)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_RecordingQualityHeader">Recording Quality (own recordings)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_SampleRateLabel">Sample Rate (Hz):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_BitsPerSampleLabel">Bits/Sample:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_RecordingQualityHint">iRacing itself plays spotter callouts at 5512 Hz / 8-bit (the classic radio sound). iRacing does not change the quality of your files, so use the format you want the final sound to have.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_SquelchHeader">Radio Squelch (Noise/Click)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_SquelchAutoAdd">Add automatically</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_SquelchDurationLabel">Duration (ms):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_SquelchVolumeLabel">Volume (0-1):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_SquelchHint">Adds a short noise/click burst at the start and end of every generated file, like a typical radio squelch tail.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_PttHeader">Roger Beep (End of Transmission Signal)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_PttAutoAdd">Add automatically</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_PttDurationLabel">Duration (ms):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_PttVolumeLabel">Volume (0-1):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_PttEndFrequencyLabel">Beep tone (Hz):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_PttEndFileLabel">Custom beep file:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_PttHint">Adds a short two-tone "Roger Beep" right after the end of every generated file, like a real radio operator releasing the push-to-talk (PTT) button to signal "transmission over". Real radios don't have an audible start signal (only the receiver's squelch opening, see above), so no tone is added at the start. Can be enabled independently of the radio squelch above. Optionally, a custom WAV file can be used instead of the generated tone.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_Test">Test</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_TestAll">Test all together</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_TestHint">Plays a short synthetic test tone using the current (not yet saved) settings, so you can hear changes immediately.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_TestRecordingLabel">Custom test take:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_TestRecordingHint">Optional: record your own test phrase or pick an existing WAV file. If a test take is present, the Test buttons use it instead of the synthetic test tone.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_RadioEffectHeader">Radio Effect (Bandpass/Distortion)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_RadioEffectAutoAdd">Apply automatically</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_RadioEffectLowCutLabel">Low cut (Hz):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_RadioEffectHighCutLabel">High cut (Hz):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_RadioEffectDistortionLabel">Distortion (0-1):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_RadioEffectHint">Applies a bandpass filter (cuts frequencies outside the chosen range) plus optional soft-clip distortion to every generated file, giving it that typical narrow, slightly overdriven "radio transmission" character.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_OutputVolumeHeader">Output Volume</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_OutputVolumeLabel">Volume (1.0 = unchanged):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Str_OutputVolumeHint">Boosts or reduces the overall volume of every generated file. Increase this if the messages sound too quiet in iRacing (values above 1.0 may introduce slight clipping).</sys:String>
